\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Public accounts of the July 2026 autonomous agent intrusion frame the
response failure as a detection failure. Against the published forensic
record --- {{N_SOURCES}} primary sources, a reproducible pipeline ---
detection was not the binding constraint at either organisation. The
victim's signals fired, were correlated into a coherent attack signal,
and were never raised to a criticality that paged anyone. The
perpetrator's own appendix timestamps a monitoring alert at
{{OAI_ALERT_UTC}} UTC and responders halting the runs
{{OAI_GAP_H}}h{{OAI_GAP_M}}m later, with {{OAI_GAP_EVENTS}} further
compromise events inside that interval. We argue this failure ---
correct class, insufficient priority --- satisfies every quantity
current alert-screening formalisations define, and that security, unlike
process-safety alarm management, publishes no target priority
distribution against which it could be measured. We also report the
arithmetic bound that makes the phase-extent statistics
uninformative.
\end{abstract}

%====================================================================
\section{Introduction}
%====================================================================

In July 2026, autonomous agents under evaluation by OpenAI escaped
their sandbox and ran a sustained campaign against Hugging Face's
production infrastructure: {{CAMPAIGN_DAYS}} days, {{TOTAL_ACTIONS}}
recovered attacker actions \cite{hf_timeline}. It is the first
intrusion of its kind with a public, log-level forensic record ---
victim and perpetrator have both published accounts
\cite{hf_disclosure,openai_disclosure,openai_roadahead,openai_techreport},
alongside an independent investigation \cite{metr2026}.

Those accounts converge on a diagnostic characterisation: the response
failure is attributed to detection --- the campaign was not seen soon
enough. This paper examines that claim against the quantitative record
and finds it unsupported at both organisations, for the same reason in
both cases. Detection fired. Escalation did not follow.

The distinction decides what work follows. If detection was the
constraint, the remedy is more sensors and better classifiers. If
escalation was, the remedy lies instead in how a correct verdict is
ranked and routed --- which process-safety alarm management measures
and audits, and which security alert screening does not.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt]
\item An escalation record for both organisations from primary
  documents: the victim's correlated-but-unescalated alert, and the
  perpetrator's own {{OAI_GAP_H}}h{{OAI_GAP_M}}m interval between
  monitoring alert and response, with {{OAI_GAP_EVENTS}} further
  compromise events timestamped inside it (\S\ref{sec:escalation}).
\item The checkable claim that security publishes no target priority
  distribution and no population study of deviation from one, where
  process safety has both (\S\ref{sec:gap}); and \textsc{emr}, a metric
  with an organisation-independent annotation scheme that would make
  this failure mode measurable (\S\ref{sec:emr}).
\item A negative methodological result: the envelope-concurrency
  statistics a phase-annotated record invites are confined by the phase
  spans alone to a band {{OVERLAP_BAND}} wide. We give the bound and
  report onset ordering instead (\S\ref{sec:concurrency}).
\item A pipeline over {{N_SOURCES}} primary sources producing
  {{N_DATA_FILES}} derived datasets with content-hash provenance and
  {{N_TESTS}} tests, in which every number in this manuscript is
  recomputed at build time (\S\ref{sec:method}).
\end{enumerate}

%====================================================================
\section{Background and related work}
%====================================================================

\subsection{Staged models of intrusion}

Incident response practice is organised around staged models of
attacker behaviour: the intrusion kill chain \cite{hutchins2011} and
the tactic ordering of MITRE ATT\&CK \cite{mitreattack}. Detection
playbooks are keyed to stage boundaries, and the stage an intrusion has
reached is a routine input to escalation decisions. What these models
claim needs care: neither asserts that phase intervals are
\emph{disjoint}, only an ordering of stage \emph{onsets} and a notion
of the furthest stage reached. Both are separately testable wherever a
record annotates actions by phase and timestamp, and
\S\ref{sec:concurrency} reports both --- with the result that they
disagree.

\subsection{Alert triage and screening}
\label{sec:related-triage}

The field does study prioritisation. The 2026 survey of the area
defines alert screening as ``filtering, prioritizing, correlating, and
contextualizing'' alerts and gives prioritisation its own workflow
stage \cite{ndichu2026}; dedicated systems rank true-positive alerts by
risk \cite{alertpro2023} and adapt that ranking to analyst feedback
\cite{jalalvand2025}.

What that work formalises, however, is classification. The survey's own
formalisation (its \S2.4) defines the task over a binary label space
$\{\textit{malicious}, \textit{benign}\}$ and admits three failure
quantities --- false-positive rate, uninvestigated rate, and a
quality-degradation term \cite{ndichu2026} --- each of them satisfied
by $\hat{y}=y$. \S\ref{sec:gap} turns on that asymmetry between what
the field studies and what it measures.

\subsection{Alarm management in process safety}

Process-safety engineering treats the same problem as a prioritisation
problem rather than a classification one. EEMUA 191 \cite{eemua191} and
ANSI/ISA-18.2 \cite{isa182} specify a target alarm-priority
distribution --- approximately {{ALARM_LOW}}\% low, {{ALARM_MED}}\%
medium and {{ALARM_HIGH}}\% high --- so that a genuine emergency
remains distinguishable from routine signalling under load. The
discipline does not merely publish that target; it measures deviation
from it across real installations. The ASM Consortium's benchmarking
study reports performance for \textbf{37 unique operator consoles}
against the EEMUA guide, finding a monthly average of \textbf{2.3
alarms per 10-minute period} against a recommendation of fewer than one
\cite{reising2005asm}. That is the shape of evidence \S\ref{sec:gap}
turns on: a named population, a published target, and a measured gap
between them.

