\section{Error analysis}
\label{sec:corrections}
%====================================================================

\subsection{The phase annotation is not a partition}

Per-phase totals sum to {{PHASE_SUM}} against {{TOTAL_ACTIONS}}
recorded actions, a shortfall of {{PHASE_GAP}} actions
({{PHASE_GAP_PCT}}\%). The multi-tagging explanation that appears in
secondary summaries of this incident is inconsistent with the data:
assigning several tags to one action drives the sum \emph{above} the
total, not below it. Either tags attach to action clusters rather than
actions, or a residual fraction is unclassified; the source does not
disambiguate. Phase annotations are therefore treated here as a
non-exhaustive labelling, and no result depends on their being a
partition. A second error in the inherited record, a quotation
attributed to the wrong post, is in \ref{app:prov}.

%====================================================================
\section{Discussion}
%====================================================================

\subsection{A representational gap}
\label{sec:gap}

The failure that determined this incident's outcome was neither a
missed detection nor a false positive: the alert existed and its class
was correct, its priority was not, and no escalation followed. Within
the binary label space of the field's formalisation \cite{ndichu2026}
that event is indistinguishable from a success --- a malicious sample
labelled malicious, with every failure quantity defined satisfied by
$\hat{y}=y$.

The claim has to be put precisely, because the obvious stronger version
is false. Security \emph{does} study prioritisation
(\S\ref{sec:related-triage}); what that work optimises is ranking
\emph{quality}. What it does not supply --- and what no security source
we could find supplies --- is a \emph{target} against which a ranking
can be called wrong, or a study measuring real deployments against one.
Process safety has had both for decades
\cite{eemua191,isa182,reising2005asm}. The gap is not that security
ignores priority, nor that the problem is hard; it is the absence of a
published benchmark distribution and of the population study a
benchmark makes possible. We do not claim Hugging Face's alarm
configuration departs from the
{{ALARM_LOW}}/{{ALARM_MED}}/{{ALARM_HIGH}} target, which is
unpublished: the comparison is between two literatures, not between an
organisation and a standard.

\subsection{A metric that would make the failure visible}
\label{sec:emr}

A benchmark whose label space is binary cannot represent the failure
that decided this incident, so no measured progress against it would
have prevented the outcome. The extension required is small, and
changes the annotation rather than the model class.

Let an alert carry a true class $y \in \{m, b\}$ and, when $y = m$, a
true urgency tier $u \in \{1,\dots,P\}$ assigned as ISA-18.2
rationalisation assigns one, from consequence severity and the time
available to respond \cite{isa182}. Let a screener emit
$(\hat{y},\hat{u})$, and $u^{*}$ be the tier at which an
organisation's policy triggers out-of-band escalation. Alongside the
rates already defined \cite{ndichu2026}, define the
\emph{escalation-miss rate}
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{EMR} = \Pr\!\left(\hat{u} < u^{*} \,\middle|\,
  y = m,\; \hat{y} = m,\; u \geq u^{*}\right),
\end{equation}
the proportion of correctly classified malicious alerts ranked below
the escalation threshold --- invisible to every metric currently
reported, each satisfied by $\hat{y}=y$. It must be reported with its
dual, the escalation-\emph{promotion} rate over alerts with
$u < u^{*}$: otherwise a screener drives EMR to zero by promoting
everything, reproducing the saturation that makes under-prioritisation
rational under load. That is why the process-safety target is a
\emph{distribution} rather than a floor on high-priority recall.

Since $u^{*}$ is organisation-specific, a bare tier $u$ would not
transfer between deployments; \ref{app:emr} gives the
organisation-independent annotation that fixes this.

%====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
%====================================================================

The July 2026 intrusion is widely described as a campaign defeated by
insufficient detection. The public record does not support that
description. At the victim, signals fired and were correlated into a
coherent attack signal that was never raised to a criticality
triggering on-call; at the perpetrator, a true-positive monitoring
alert preceded the response by
{{OAI_GAP_H}}~hours~{{OAI_GAP_M}}~minutes, with {{OAI_GAP_EVENTS}}
further compromise events in between. The binding constraint at both
organisations was the step from a correct verdict to a human interrupt.
Process safety benchmarks that error against a published target
distribution; security has neither the target nor the population study.
We propose the metric and annotation that would permit both, and
release the corpus, pipeline and harness so they can be applied to the
next such record.

\section{Limitations and Dual-Use Considerations}
\label{sec:limits}

\paragraph{The two findings are not equally well evidenced.} The
escalation result is the firm one: both halves are primary --- a direct
quotation from the victim \cite{hf_timeline}, and two timestamps in
OpenAI's own appendix \cite{openai_techreport} located by anchor string
in a hashed copy --- so a reader who rejects all secondary sourcing
keeps \S\ref{sec:escalation} intact. The concurrency result is weaker,
for the two reasons given where it is reported (L3, and the bound of
\S\ref{sec:conc-method}); a reader who discounts
\S\ref{sec:concurrency} entirely is left with the escalation finding
and the representational argument it motivates, which is unaffected.

\paragraph{Further limitations.}
\begin{description}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt,style=unboxed]
\item[(L1) Single incident.] All results describe one campaign. No
inferential statistic is reported and none would be appropriate;
nothing here establishes a base rate.

\item[(L2) Derived data.] The raw action log and agent transcripts are
unreleased, and aggregation choices in the published summary are the
publisher's and cannot be audited from outside.

\item[(L3) Envelopes are not duty cycles.] Concurrency is estimated
from interval endpoints, which is why inference is restricted to dense
phases (\S\ref{sec:conc-method}, \ref{app:conc}) and the unrestricted
figure is reported alongside. Simultaneous \emph{activity} is not
established by this paper.

\item[(L4) Secondary-sourced dates.] Two events --- OpenAI's internal
identification and the 5 August conference disclosure --- rest on
anonymously-sourced press reporting. {{N_EVENTS_VERIFIED}} of
{{N_EVENTS_TOTAL}} events are anchored in cached primary sources, and
every dependent interval is marked (Table~\ref{tab:lags}).

\item[(L5) Paywalled standards.] The
{{ALARM_LOW}}/{{ALARM_MED}}/{{ALARM_HIGH}} target is corroborated
across independent industry sources rather than retrieved from EEMUA
191 or ISA-18.2 directly.

\item[(L6) Verification establishes consistency, not correctness.] The
build guarantees that reported values match the artifacts they derive
from. It cannot detect an inappropriate measure or a mis-specified
source.

\item[(L7) EMR is unmeasured here.] Hugging Face's escalation policy
and alarm configuration are unpublished, so no value of EMR is claimed
(\ref{app:emr}); the metric is an instrument, not a measurement.
\end{description}

\paragraph{Dual use.} This paper reports aggregate campaign structure
and timing derived from already-public material. It contains no
exploit, payload or capability uplift; the captured commands it draws
on were published by the victim with credentials and hostnames
redacted; no novel installation recipe, intrusion technique or evasion
method is described; and nothing withheld by the primary sources is
reconstructed. The findings are defensive, arguing for changes to alert
prioritisation and to the evaluation of screening systems. The
foreseeable misuse --- an adversary inferring that interleaved phases
degrade stage-keyed playbooks --- carries low marginal risk, since that
interleaving is present in the victim's own published artifact. The
released repository contains parsers and derived aggregates only.

\paragraph{LLM-assisted authoring.} The author used Claude Opus 5 for
source retrieval, prose drafting and code review. The research
question, the density-restricted concurrency measure, the escalation
reconstruction, the comparison between the two literatures, the
ingestion and verification design, and all judgements about what the
evidence supports are the author's own. All quotations were retrieved
from primary sources and are located by page or anchor.

