\section{The concurrency measure and its bound}
\label{app:conc}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
{{PHASE_FIGURE_TEX}}
\caption{Phase envelopes over normalised campaign time, ordered by
onset; action counts at right. Dark bars are the {{DENSE_COUNT}} dense
phases, which begin within {{DENSE_ONSET_SPREAD}}\% of one another and
run to the campaign's end. Light bars are the {{SPARSE_COUNT}} sparse
phases, whose onsets are ordered and extend to {{ONSET_LAST}}\%.
Generated from the data, not drawn by hand.}
\label{fig:phases}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Definitions.} With campaign time normalised to $[0,1]$ and
$c_D(t)=\left|\{p \in D: t \in E_p\}\right|$ the number of dense phases
whose envelope contains $t$, the coextensivity fraction is
\begin{equation}
\Phi_D = \mu\left(\{t \in [0,1] : c_D(t) = |D|\}\right),
\end{equation}
estimated on a uniform grid of $10^3$ points. Overlap $O(a,b)$ is
normalised by the shorter envelope so that a short phase wholly
contained in a longer one scores $1$ rather than being diluted by the
longer phase's extent.

\paragraph{The bound.} Two intervals in $[0,1]$ with spans
$\sigma_a,\sigma_b$ intersect on at least $\max(0,\sigma_a+\sigma_b-1)$,
so
\begin{equation}
O(a,b) \;\geq\; \frac{\max(0,\;\sigma_a+\sigma_b-1)}{\min(\sigma_a,\sigma_b)},
\qquad
1-\!\!\sum_{p\in D}(1-\sigma_p) \;\leq\; \Phi_D \;\leq\; \min_{p \in D}\sigma_p .
\end{equation}
The spans of Table~\ref{tab:phases} therefore fix $\overline{O}$ within
{{OVERLAP_BAND}} and $\Phi_D$ within {{PHI_BAND}}~pp before any overlap
is computed.

\paragraph{Two limits on the density restriction.} First, $\tau =
{{DENSE_MIN_ACTIONS}}$ is a round number chosen to separate the three
phases of order $10^3$ actions from the six of order $10^2$. The gap in
the data is wide enough that any threshold in $[200, 2900]$ yields the
same set, so no sensitivity analysis over $\tau$ would be informative
--- the partition is nowhere near a boundary. Second, and more
seriously, $\delta_p$ is a \emph{mean} rate over an envelope, and a
mean rate constrains the distribution within it only under
near-uniformity. The distribution here is not near-uniform:
{{BUSIEST_DAY}} carries {{BUSIEST_SHARE}}\% of all recorded actions. A
phase whose actions are concentrated in a short burst with stragglers
at each end yields the same envelope and the same density while being
inactive for most of it. Nothing in the released summary excludes this,
because the summary publishes per-phase envelopes and per-day totals
--- the two marginals of a phase-by-day table whose interior is not
public. Duty cycles are therefore not computable from this artifact,
and \S\ref{sec:concurrency} reports extent as a bound rather than as
activity.

\paragraph{What the bound retires.} Neither statistic is evidence for
concurrency; each is a restatement of the span column. The claim that a
strictly staged campaign implies $O(a,b)\approx 0$ remains true, but it
is not a claim these spans could have falsified. Any reanalysis of a
phase-annotated record should compute this bound before reporting an
overlap statistic.

\section{Source provenance, validation and parser failure modes}
\label{app:prov}

\paragraph{Provenance.} The ASM Consortium benchmarking paper
\cite{reising2005asm} is distributed by Honeywell at
\texttt{process.honeywell.com} under
\texttt{/content/dam/process/en/documents/document-lists/}
\texttt{doc\_asm-consortium/white-papers/}. Each of the {{N_SOURCES}}
artifacts is recorded
in \texttt{cache/manifest.json} with URL, SHA-256, byte length, HTTP
status, retrieval timestamp and evidential class: \textsc{primary} for
victim or perpetrator publications, \textsc{independent} for
third-party investigation, \textsc{secondary} for press reporting. The
class travels with the data rather than being remembered, so a claim's
evidential status is recomputed at build time from the source it was
drawn from.

\paragraph{Two artifacts that appear unavailable and are not.} Requests
without a browser User-Agent header receive HTTP 403 from
\texttt{huggingface.co}, \texttt{openai.com} and \texttt{metr.org};
with such a header every source returns HTTP 200. The block is a
User-Agent filter, not access control, and reading it as the latter is
what led an earlier assessment of this incident's evidence base to
record two live sources as unavailable. Separately, the interactive
replay has no companion dataset repository because its data is embedded
directly in its 43.9\,kB \texttt{index.html} as JavaScript literals ---
a file that reads as an empty shell is in fact the dataset. Both
properties are recorded here because any independent reanalysis will
meet them first.

\paragraph{A quotation attributed to the wrong document.} Secondary
compilations of this incident attribute a passage on frontier-model
refusal during forensic analysis to Hugging Face's technical timeline.
The passage appears in the disclosure \cite{hf_disclosure}; the
technical timeline carries a distinct statement on the same subject
\cite{hf_timeline}. Both documents contain a section headed ``the
asymmetry problem'', which plausibly accounts for the conflation. Every
quotation in this paper is therefore located by anchor string within a
specific cached artifact, and the locator is released with the data.

\paragraph{Extraction yields.} The investigation PDF yields
{{METR_QUOTES}} page-numbered agent-reasoning excerpts,
{{METR_PASSAGES}} quoted passages and {{N_AGENT_IDS}} distinct agent
pseudonyms across {{METR_PAGES}} pages. The technical timeline yields
{{HF_COMMAND_BLOCKS}} captured command blocks and a section tree with
every declared quotation anchor located. OpenAI's technical report
yields {{OAI_TR_ENTRIES}} timestamped appendix entries across
{{OAI_TR_PAGES}} pages. The released harness contains {{N_TESTS}}
tests, all offline, over {{N_DATA_FILES}} derived data files.

\paragraph{Anchor-located timestamps.} The two OpenAI escalation
endpoints are located in the report's appendix by anchor substring
rather than row index, and the pipeline raises if an anchor matches
anything other than exactly one row. A revised or reordered appendix
therefore fails loudly instead of silently yielding an interval
computed from a neighbouring pair of events.

\paragraph{Parser failure modes.} The captured commands contain two
constructs that defeat naive lexical extraction: URLs whose
\texttt{//} is indistinguishable from a comment introducer, and
double-quoted strings containing single quotes. Extraction by pattern
substitution truncates such records without error, producing a
well-formed dataset that is silently incomplete --- the failure mode
here is not a crash but a plausible, wrong dataset. The released
implementation therefore parses by scanning with explicit string-state
tracking, and both constructs are covered by regression tests. This is
the principal data-integrity risk for any independent reanalysis of
this artifact.

\section{What the incident establishes about EMR, and further recommendations}
\label{app:emr}

\paragraph{The incident motivates EMR; it does not measure it.} EMR
conditions on $u \geq u^{*}$, and $u^{*}$ is a property of the
deploying organisation's escalation policy. Hugging Face's policy and
alarm configuration are unpublished and not recoverable from any source
used here, so we cannot establish that the un-escalated alert satisfied
$u \geq u^{*}$ and therefore cannot claim EMR took any particular
value. What the record supports is weaker and still sufficient: an
alert was correctly classified, was not raised to a criticality that
triggered on-call, and the organisation's own remediation was to couple
severity to paging \cite{hf_disclosure} --- an acknowledgement that the
priority assigned was below the threshold that should have applied. The
ASM benchmarking design \cite{reising2005asm} shows what
consequence-and-response-window labels then permit: a population study
of measured deviation from a published target, which is the form of
evidence security alert screening currently cannot produce.

\paragraph{Making the annotation organisation-independent.} Since
$u^{*}$ is a property of the deploying organisation, a bare tier $u$
would not transfer between deployments. The fix is to record what the
tier is derived from rather than the tier itself: annotate each
malicious alert with the pair (consequence class, response window) that
ISA-18.2 rationalisation already uses as its inputs \cite{isa182}, both
of which are properties of the event. Each deploying organisation then
supplies its own map from that pair to a tier, and its own $u^{*}$. The
corpus stays portable, the threshold stays local, and EMR is computed
per deployment and compared across deployments only through the
published target distribution. The adoption cost is annotation, not
modelling.

\paragraph{Do not condition agentic response on the attacker's stage
being \emph{exclusive}.} Onsets are ordered and recoverable, so ``has
this reached RCE yet?'' has a well-defined answer here and stage-keyed
branching keeps its input. What fails is any control that treats the
current stage as the \emph{only} one running --- tearing down
reconnaissance monitoring once exploitation is confirmed, or freeing
resources allocated to an earlier stage on the assumption it has
concluded. In this campaign reconnaissance, exploitation and staging
were all still active at the final recorded action. Branch on the
furthest stage reached; do not assume the earlier ones have stopped.

\paragraph{Publish derived forensic summaries.} The replay artifact is
the entire quantitative basis for this analysis. Releasing an aggregate
summary imposes little disclosure cost --- it carries no credentials,
hostnames or exploit detail --- and it is what makes independent
analysis possible when the raw logs cannot be released. Publishing the
phase-by-day interior alongside the two marginals would, at no
additional disclosure cost, convert the extent bound of
\S\ref{sec:conc-method} into a duty-cycle measurement.

\section{Reproduction}
\label{app:repro}

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
pip install -r requirements.txt
python ingest/run_all.py       # fetch, hash and parse all sources
python analysis/concurrency.py # phase density and concurrency
python analysis/escalation.py  # escalation intervals
python verify.py               # recompute all claims; render manuscript
python -m pytest tests -q      # {{N_TESTS}} tests, offline
\end{lstlisting}

\noindent
Environment: Python {{PYTHON_VERSION}} with one runtime dependency,
\texttt{pypdf=={{PYPDF_VERSION}}} (\texttt{requirements.txt}), and
\texttt{pytest} for the harness (\texttt{requirements-dev.txt}). The
pipeline is deterministic and seeds nothing; everything after ingestion
runs offline. \texttt{verify.py} recomputes every claim from
\texttt{data/} and substitutes it into this manuscript's template; an
unknown placeholder is a build failure and a computed-but-unused claim
is reported as an orphan. The manuscript has no numbers of its own, so
it cannot disagree with its artifacts.

